# Entity-Disjoint Validation Splits

Five validation designs built from `clean.csv` (3,003 aptamer–protein pairs: 809 positive, 2,194 negative), ranging from a standard random split to a doubly-disjoint split in which neither the aptamer nor the protein in any test pair appears in training.

Entity identity is recovered by fingerprinting each row's 340-dimensional k-mer sub-vector (aptamer) and 300-dimensional PseAAC sub-vector (protein), since the feature table stores only descriptors, not sequence identifiers. Identical fingerprints indicate the same physical entity. 803 unique aptamer entities and 508 unique protein entities were recovered from the 3,003 pairs.

The original M1/M2/M3 cohort construction is itself an unconstrained stratified random split, and is therefore statistically equivalent to the pair-level design below; it is not listed as a separate design to avoid duplicating identical data.

## Designs

| # | Design | File prefix | Entity control |
|---|--------|-------------|-----------------|
| 1 | Pair-level (random) | `pair_level_*` | None — standard random stratified split |
| 2 | Protein-disjoint | `protein_disjoint_*` | Test proteins absent from training |
| 3 | Aptamer-disjoint | `aptamer_disjoint_*` | Test aptamers absent from training |
| 4 | Cluster-disjoint | `cluster_disjoint_*` | Aptamer similarity clusters (cosine ≥ 0.90) disjoint between train and test |
| 5 | Doubly-disjoint | `doubly_disjoint_*` | Neither aptamer nor protein entity shared between train and test |

## File manifest

```
splits/
├── README.md
├── split_summary.csv                  one row per design: train/test counts and class balance
├── kmer4_correction_map.csv           256-row k=4 label correction map
│
├── pair_level_train.csv / pair_level_test.csv
├── protein_disjoint_train.csv / protein_disjoint_test.csv
├── aptamer_disjoint_train.csv / aptamer_disjoint_test.csv
├── cluster_disjoint_train.csv / cluster_disjoint_test.csv
├── doubly_disjoint_train.csv / doubly_disjoint_test.csv
│
├── doubly_disjoint_detail/
│   ├── doubly_disjoint_audit.csv      per-pair assignment (dev/test/discarded) with entity IDs
│   └── doubly_disjoint_results.json   metrics, confidence intervals, leakage verification
│
└── all_designs_results/
    ├── Table3_all_designs.csv         comparative results table across all five designs
    └── all_results.json               full metrics, CIs, and controls for all five designs
```

Every split CSV has 641 columns: `Class` (binary label) plus 340 k-mer features (k = 1–4) and 300 PseAAC features (groups A–F).

## Split sizes

Target test fraction 0.20, seed 42.

| Design | Train | Test | Test positive | Test negative |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Pair-level | 2,402 | 601 | 162 | 439 |
| Protein-disjoint | 2,386 | 617 | 155 | 462 |
| Aptamer-disjoint | 2,365 | 638 | 144 | 494 |
| Cluster-disjoint | 2,376 | 627 | 160 | 467 |
| Doubly-disjoint | 1,324 | 345 | 109 | 236 |

The doubly-disjoint construction discards 1,334 pairs (44.4%) in which one entity falls in the development pool and the other in the test pool; such pairs cannot be assigned to either side without leaking an entity across the boundary. This is expected given the entity density of the dataset.

## Doubly-disjoint leakage verification

```
Dev  aptamers / proteins : 365 / 274
Test aptamers / proteins : 136 / 108
Leaking aptamers : 0
Leaking proteins : 0
Status : PASS
```

Full audit trail: `doubly_disjoint_detail/doubly_disjoint_audit.csv`.

## Results

ExtraTrees (500 estimators), MCC-optimal threshold selected on 5-fold out-of-fold cross-validation, evaluated once on each held-out test set:

| Design | N test | MCC | MCC 95% CI | ROC-AUC | Accuracy | Recall | Specificity | Memorisation-control MCC | Permutation-control AUC |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Pair-level | 601 | 0.8801 | [0.833, 0.916] | 0.9506 | 0.9534 | 0.858 | 0.9886 | 0.218 | 0.503 |
| Protein-disjoint | 617 | 0.8825 | [0.842, 0.925] | 0.9606 | 0.9562 | 0.839 | 0.9957 | 0.033 | 0.550 |
| Aptamer-disjoint | 638 | 0.0704 | [0.014, 0.111] | 0.6248 | 0.2586 | 0.986 | 0.0466 | −0.073 | 0.468 |
| Cluster-disjoint | 627 | −0.0318 | [−0.116, 0.038] | 0.6013 | 0.2552 | 0.994 | 0.0021 | −0.068 | 0.468 |
| Doubly-disjoint | 345 | 0.0226 | [−0.089, 0.109] | 0.4956 | 0.3362 | 0.963 | 0.0466 | 0.062 | 0.524 |

Out-of-fold cross-validation MCC on the development partition remains in the 0.84–0.88 range across all five designs; the designs diverge only at test time.

**Interpretation.** Protein identity carries little memorisable signal: protein-disjoint test performance (MCC 0.883) is statistically indistinguishable from the pair-level baseline (0.880). Aptamer identity is the dominant source of apparent performance under conventional evaluation — once aptamer entities are held out (aptamer-disjoint, cluster-disjoint, doubly-disjoint), test MCC collapses to 0.02–0.07, within or near the chance range, while recall remains near ceiling and specificity collapses toward zero, indicating the classifier defaults to the positive class once it cannot recognise the specific aptamer. The entity-memorisation control — a predictor using no sequence information, only each entity's training-set positive rate — tracks the same pattern, falling from MCC 0.22 under pair-level evaluation to near zero or negative under any aptamer-disjoint design, independently confirming that pair-level performance is substantially attributable to entity recurrence rather than learned interaction signal. The label-permutation control remains at chance (AUC ≈ 0.45–0.55) across every design, confirming the evaluation pipeline itself introduces no artefactual signal.

These results indicate that reported accuracy under the primary cohort design should be read alongside the aptamer-disjoint and doubly-disjoint figures as the more realistic estimate of generalisation to previously unseen aptamers.

## k=4 label correction

The k=4 column labels in `clean.csv`, `train.csv`, `M1.csv`, `M2.csv`, and `M3.csv` have positions 3–4 (0-indexed 2–3) transposed relative to the sequence whose frequency they store — for example, the column `aptamer_frequency_GGGC` holds the frequency of `GGCG`. 192 of 256 possible 4-mer labels are affected; the remaining 64 are palindromic under the swap and unaffected. Feature values are correct; only column names require renaming.

Corrected copies are provided at the repository root, named by data role:

| Original | Corrected | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `clean.csv` | `clean_kmer_corrected.csv` | Full feature table |
| `train.csv` (= `M1.csv`) | `train_kmer_corrected.csv` | Development partition |
| `M2.csv` | `val_kmer_corrected.csv` | Semi-unseen validation cohort |
| `M3.csv` | `test_kmer_corrected.csv` | External unseen test cohort |

`kmer4_correction_map.csv` documents every stored-to-correct label pair.

## Reproducing these results

```bash
pip install numpy pandas scikit-learn

python build_entity_disjoint_splits.py --data clean.csv --out splits/
python doubly_disjoint_analysis.py --data clean.csv --out splits/doubly_disjoint_detail
python kmer_label_correction.py --fix --data clean.csv train.csv M2.csv M3.csv
python run_all_validation_designs.py --data clean.csv --out splits/all_designs_results
```

All scripts default to `--seed 42` for exact reproducibility.
